-        "note": "THE START IS THE COMPLETION OF A BUILDING SOMEBODY ELSE BEGAN. Andreas's chronology enters \"'Cobweb Castle,' completed by Dr. Wolcott\" against 1820, the year Wolcott was appointed agent, and his narrative says Wolcott 'finished and resided in a building commenced during Judge Jouett's incumbency' — Jouett was agent 1816-18. So the fabric is older than 1820 by up to four years and this range does not claim the earlier part. THE FORM THIS RECORD MODELS IS LATER STILL: the centre, two wings and two tails are Wau-Bun's description of 1831, and Wau-Bun says in as many words that the wings and tails were ADDITIONS made when 'there was found no more room for additions at the sides'. A record that split those additions into phases would have to invent their dates; instead the phase runs from 1820 and carries the 1831 form throughout, which overstates the building's size in the 1820s. THE END OF THE RANGE IS THE HONEST MINIMUM AND IT IS THE WEAKEST THING HERE. No source reached gives this building a fate. It is on this project's own watch_list in data/exclusions.json for that reason: standing and in civilian occupation in the autumn of 1832, when Andreas has Colonel Hamilton residing in 'the old Indian agency-house', and nothing after. The agency itself wound down through the 1833 treaty and the 1835 removal, and an agency house whose agency has gone is exactly the kind of building that comes down without anyone writing it down. Presence on 1835-07-01 is an inference from continuity, not a finding. If evidence turns up that it was gone, this record leaves the scene and moves to data/exclusions.json.",
